  "findings": [
    "A frame may already expose fixed Date.now and Date constructor while its original 17 stock expiry dates were initialized from the actual Nov10 date.",
    "page.clock alone reproduced the race once in ten loads; it is not a proven stable replacement.",
    "Reapplying the same ReviewDate clock after Document.open also reproduced the race once in ten loads; its missing open-hook events on that load support a late init-script/early srcdoc bootstrap ordering issue.",
    "The native-CDP page-target experiment did not control the opaque child date and is not a usable replacement."
  ],
  "limits": [
    "Not a definitive Chromium-internal root cause diagnosis.",
    "Ten samples per method do not establish a flake rate or clock stability.",
    "No stable permanent replacement was demonstrated."
  ],
  "recommended_current_scope": "Preserve failed runs. Keep exact baseline world/date comparison as a required gate; report a final run only when all functional cases and all baseline dates/worlds match. Do not seed or normalize the wrong world dates, and do not treat a late fixed Date.now assertion as proof of correct initialization."
